From 5f1df62ca809abe2b80a194f671bf518ae31a01f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 14:43:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 15/17] Dynamic resolution of the 3D view (BFG's resolution
 scaling, wired up)

When frames are slow, FullscreenFXManager::RenderScaledScene draws the
player's view cropped to a smaller size (CropRenderSize: the front end
computes viewport, light scissors and window coordinates for it) and
captures it into the image _scaledSceneImage; Process stretches it over
the screen with the material _scaledScene (opaque; translucent so it is
not depth tested EQUAL in the 2D view). The HUD, texts and menus drawn
after it stay at the full size. RenderScene no longer scales by itself
(BFG scaled every 3D view there and stretched none): it draws into the
crop it is given, so demos, screenshots and the end of a level keep the
full size. More crops than renderCrops holds are an error, not a write
past it.

The scale is taken once a frame, when its command buffer starts
(SwapCommandBuffers_FinishCommandBuffers, the game thread stopped):
with com_smp the back end steers it while the game thread draws, and
two views of one frame reading it apart could crop and stretch by two
sizes. A portal sky is drawn and captured at the view's size, which is
the size the view reads it back at. Only views that keep to their
viewport are scaled: with antialiasing, HDR, SSAO, SSGI or motion blur
(passes over the whole screen), or while a demo is recorded, the scale
stays 1. The back end's filmic post-processing stays within the view;
PostProcess no longer runs for rs_enable alone.

rs_enable 4: both axes alike; rs_minimum: the smallest scale. Without
GPU timer queries (Mesa: off in RBDOOM, and panfrost's split batches)
idResolutionScale::SetCurrentFrameTime steers by the time between
frames and by whether the GPU holds them back: GL_BlockingSwapBuffers
looks (without waiting) at the fence of the frame before when this one
has been issued; if the GPU is still on it, the GPU is the bottleneck.
The scale drops proportionally when frames take rs_frameDropMilliseconds
longer than the engine's rate while the GPU holds them back; when the
CPU does, fewer pixels would only blur the view (the game pinned to the
two A53 cores: full size at 56 fps). It rises a step after waiting at
the full rate, or with the GPU idle while the CPU holds the frames back,
waiting longer after a rise that had to be taken back. (Judging each
drop by the frames after it failed in the intro: a camera cut during
the judgement took a good drop back, 18 fps at the full size for
seconds.)

Handhelds: rs_enable 4, rs_minimum 0.6. Mali-G52 (854x480): the start of
mars_city2 27.8 -> 46 fps, the mars_city1 hangar 34 -> 54 fps; light
scenes keep the full size at 60 fps.
